## Title
Graph Neural Network Enhanced Finite Element Modelling

## Authors
Rutwik Gulakala, Bernd Markert, Marcus Stoffel
*Institute of General Mechanics (IAM), RWTH Aachen University, Aachen, Germany*

## Abstract
A Graph Neural Network (GNN)-enhanced Finite Element Method (FEM) framework is proposed to accelerate structural simulations. The approach uses discretized FE geometry as a graph input, learns boundary value problem dynamics via message-passing, and predicts von-Mises stress distributions at each node — with outputs readable by standard FE post-processors.

## Keywords
- Graph Neural Networks (GNN)
- Finite Element Method (FEM)
- von-Mises stress prediction
- Message-passing neural networks
- Structural mechanics
- Surrogate modelling

## Research Problem
- FEM simulations are computationally expensive, especially for complex boundary value problems (e.g., crash simulations)
- Existing ML surrogates (CNN-based) are limited to structured rectangular domains and use images as I/O
- No prior GNN-FEM work directly consumes/produces FE-native nodal data (coordinates, boundary conditions, edge connectivity)

## Methodology
- **Data generation:** 540 samples from ABAQUS simulations of rectangular aluminium plates with holes at varied locations, sizes, and loads; exported via ODB2VTK pipeline
- **Material model:** Isotropic elastoplastic Johnson-Cook model
- **Graph architecture:** Undirected, homogeneous, static graph; Encoder-Decoder design
  - Encoder: Transformer convolution layer (aggregation) + MLP (message-passing)
  - Latent vector concatenated with material data, passed through FFNN
  - Decoder: MLP reconstructing nodal output features
- **Hyperparameter tuning:** Hyperband search algorithm; selected 3 transformer layers, 16 latent units, 5 MLP layers
- **Training:** 500/540 samples, 1000 epochs, Adam optimizer, MSE loss, learning rate 3E-3 with exponential decay from epoch 200

## Key Findings
- MSE of **5.89E-2** on the test set
- Absolute stress errors of **1.7E9–3.18E9 and 2.15E10–2.9E10 GPa** across geometry/loading cases
- Computational gain of **12.7%** (inclusive of training cost)
- Strong spatial correlation between GNN-predicted and FE-simulated von-Mises stress fields
- Network generalizes reasonably to extrapolation cases (geometries/loads outside training range), though with higher error

## Contributions
- First GNN-FEM approach that operates directly on FE-native nodal/edge data rather than images
- End-to-end pipeline: FE pre-processor → GNN inference → FE post-processor (VTK-compatible output)
- Incorporation of mechanics knowledge (material parameters, boundary conditions) as latent inputs to improve generalization
- Demonstrated applicability to elasto-plastic, unstructured, complex domains where CNNs fail

## Limitations
- Modest computational speedup (12.7%) — training overhead partially offsets inference gains
- Evaluated only on a relatively simple class of geometries (rectangular plates with circular holes)
- Extrapolation accuracy degrades for geometries/loads significantly outside the training distribution
- Single material model and 2D-like problem scope; generalization to 3D or other material models not demonstrated
- Test set is small (40 samples out of 540)

## Conclusion
The proposed GNN-enhanced FEM framework demonstrates that graph networks can serve as viable surrogates for FE simulations by natively consuming FE discretization data. The approach preserves elemental integrity, handles unstructured domains, and shows strong in-distribution accuracy. While the current speedup is limited, the framework establishes a promising foundation for physics-informed GNN surrogates that could scale to more complex, high-cost simulations.
